<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 9: Understanding Variability                              -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-variability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Understanding Variability</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-ch9-variability">
        <p>
            Data is never perfectly consistent: measure the same thing twice and
            you will rarely get exactly the same answer.
            Track the population in a single country over fifty years and the
            numbers will shift, sometimes smoothly, sometimes in sudden jolts.
            This chapter is about variability: what it is, where it comes from,
            and how to measure it.
            In this chapter, we ....
            [TODO] We work through the main measures of variability (range,
            variance, standard deviation, interquartile range, and least
            squares) using life expectancy data from African countries as our
            running example.
            By the end of the chapter, you will be able to choose the right
            measure for a given situation, interpret what it tells you, and
            explain what it does not.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="sec-variability-how-much">
        <title>How Much Does It Vary?</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                In Kenya's capital city, Nairobi, streets are regularly
                gridlocked.
                News reports claim that commutes regularly exceed two hours each
                way.
                But is that consistently the case, and for whom is that the case
                for?
                The answer depends entirely on how you measure it, and who you
                ask.
            </p>

            <p>
                In 2013, a World Bank survey of 14,580 households across 15
                Kenyan cities set out to answer this question systematically.
                The researchers noted that while congestion at peak hours was
                obvious to anyone on Nairobi's streets, it was simply not known
                which commuters were experiencing that congestion, or what their
                commute times actually were (Salon and Gulyani, 2019).
                In the end, the survey found that the median commute time in
                Nairobi was 30 minutes, and only 5% of those surveyed reported
                commuting an hour or longer.
                Far from a city of two-hour commutes, most Nairobi residents
                appeared to be getting to work in half an hour.
            </p>

            <p>
                But another major survey, conducted in the same year by JICA,
                found an average (mean) commute time of 49 minutes - nearly
                twice the World Bank figure.
                Both surveys measured the same city in the same year.
                The difference came down to who was sampled: the JICA survey had
                oversampled car-owning households, who tend to have longer
                motorised commutes, while the World Bank survey was more
                representative of the full population — most of whom walk or
                take a matatu.
                Measure the same thing differently, and you get a different
                answer.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <title>Which Measurement -- TODO: better title.</title>

                <p>
                    Part of the difference between JICA and the World Bank is
                    simply mean versus median.
                    Averages are pulled upward by the small number of commuters
                    with very long journeys, while medians are not.
                    JICA reported a mean of 49 minutes, and a median of 40
                    minutes; while the World Bank reported a median of 30
                    minutes, and a mean of 31 minutes.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                Even within the World Bank survey, the variation is substantial.
                Matatu commuters in Nairobi spent roughly twice as long
                travelling as walk commuters making similar trips in the same
                city.
                The median commute time conceals the fact that some people's
                journeys are short and some are long — and who experiences which
                depends on how they travel, where they live, and what time they
                leave.
            </p>

            <p>
                This is what variability looks like in practice.
                This data is in a spread of values with a centre, a range, and a
                shape, called a distribution.
                Understanding that distribution, rather than just its average,
                is what allows planners to know whether interventions are
                helping everyone or only some commuters.
                It is what allows researchers to compare across cities fairly.
                And it is what allows the data analyst to say something honest
                about what the data actually shows.
                This chapter introduces the tools for observing and measuring
                variability.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-traffic-variation-measuring">
            <title>Observing Variability in Nairobi Traffic</title>

            <p>
                The TomTom Traffic Index records journey times across Nairobi
                continuously, aggregating anonymised GPS data from millions of
                trips across the entire road network.
                In 2025, a 10 kilometre drive in the city took an average of 26
                minutes and 47 seconds.
                But that single number of the average is already hiding the
                variability in journey times.
                At 4am, the same 10 kilometres takes around 18 minutes.
                At 4pm on a weekday, it takes more than twice as long at around
                37 minutes.
                The average sits between those two extremes, but it does not
                tell you that the extremes exist, or how far apart they are.
            </p>

            <p>
                One of the simplest ways to measure variability is the
                <term>range</term>: the difference between the largest and
                smallest values.
                Here, the range of journey times across the day is roughly
                <md>
                    37 -18 = 19
                </md>
                minutes.
                This is a very useful starting point to getting an idea about
                the overall variability.
            </p>

            <p>
                However, the range only tells you only about the two most
                extreme values.
                It does not say anything about a typical journey, or how much
                most journeys deviate from the average.
                And it is sensitive to unusual extremes: one exceptionally bad
                hour can make the range look larger than it really is for most
                people.
            </p>

            <p>
                To get a fuller picture, TomTom also estimated that over the
                entirety of 2025, the average Nairobi driver lost more than four
                and a half days (109 hours) sitting in traffic beyond what those
                same journeys would have taken at free-flow speeds.
                That 109 hours is itself a summary of accumulated variability
                across the whole year: some days the roads flow freely, some
                days they do not, and the total adds up.
            </p>

            <p>
                As we have seen, averages alone do not capture the full extent
                of variability.
                The variability can also be captured through various graphics.
                For example, the heatmap below shows journey times across every
                hour of every day of the week, averaged across 2025.
                Each cell represents one two-hour slot on one day of the week;
                darker red means longer journey times, lighter pink means
                shorter.
                This is a visual representation of variability: you can see at a
                glance not just that variation exists, but that journeys on
                weekday evenings are typically the longest, and journeys on
                early Sunday mornings are typically the shortest.
                The range from the lightest cell to the darkest is visible
                without any calculation at all.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-heatmap-week">
                <caption>
                    Travel time heatmap for Nairobi across all hours and days of
                    the week, averaged across 2025.
                    Darker red indicates longer journey times; lighter pink
                    indicates shorter.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025,
                    <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_heatmap_week.png" width="100%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A heatmap with days of the week (Monday to Sunday)
                            across the top and hours of the day down the left
                            side.
                            Cells are coloured from light pink to dark red.
                            The darkest cells appear in the band from
                            approximately 5pm to 7pm on weekdays, particularly
                            Monday to Thursday.
                            Saturday and Sunday are substantially lighter
                            throughout, especially in the morning hours.
                            The 4am slot on Monday is notably light.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The same variation looks different when viewed spatially.
                The maps below show average traffic conditions during the
                morning and evening rush hours across the city.
                Not all parts of Nairobi are equally congested — the central
                business district and main arterial roads are consistently
                darker, while the outer suburbs are lighter.
                And the spatial pattern of variability shifts between morning
                and evening: congestion concentrates inward as commuters arrive
                in the morning, and spreads outward as they leave in the
                evening.
                The same underlying variation in journey times, measured as an
                average above, is now being shown as a distribution across space
                rather than across time.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-map-morning">
                <caption>
                    Traffic conditions in Nairobi during the morning rush hour,
                    averaged across 2025.
                    Yellow indicates moderate congestion, orange and red
                    indicate heavier delays.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_heatmap_morning.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A hexagonal heatmap overlaid on a dark map of
                            Nairobi.
                            The hexagons covering the central business district
                            and major roads leading into it are coloured orange
                            to red, indicating significant congestion.
                            The outer suburbs and peripheral areas are
                            predominantly yellow, indicating lighter congestion.
                            The pattern shows congestion concentrated in the
                            city centre and along main arterial routes during
                            the morning peak.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-map-evening">
                <caption>
                    Traffic conditions in Nairobi during the evening rush hour,
                    averaged across 2025.
                    Congestion spreads more broadly than in the morning,
                    extending further into residential areas as commuters make
                    their way home.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_heatmap_evening.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A hexagonal heatmap overlaid on a dark map of
                            Nairobi during the evening rush hour.
                            Compared to the morning map, the orange and red
                            hexagons extend further outward from the CBD into
                            surrounding residential neighbourhoods.
                            The overall impression is of broader, more
                            distributed congestion than the morning peak,
                            consistent with commuters dispersing in multiple
                            directions rather than converging on a single
                            centre.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                With this data, different ways of observing and summarising the
                same underlying variability in Nairobi journey times are
                possible.
                Each measurement reveals something the others do not.
                The average gives you a centre; the range tells you how far the
                extremes are from each other; the heatmap shows you where in the
                day variation is concentrated; the spatial map shows you where
                in the city it is concentrated.
                The rest of this chapter introduces the formal measures that
                complement these visual summaries, and explains when to use each
                one.
            </p>

            <insight xml:id="insight-what-you-measure">
                <title>What you measure shapes what you find</title>

                <p>
                    The TomTom figures above include Nairobi's Expressway, which
                    opened in 2022 and cut journey times on routes passing
                    through it from around two hours to roughly 20 minutes.
                    Other sources (including the Express Impress estimate of a
                    30-minute to two-hour range for a specific surface-road
                    journey in Nairobi) are measuring routes that do not use the
                    Expressway at all.
                    These two sources are measuring the same thing, and the
                    apparent variability in journey times is different depending
                    on which roads you include.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This is a general principle that applies to any dataset: the
                    variation you observe depends partly on what is actually
                    varying in the world, and partly on what you chose to
                    measure and what you left out.
                    Keeping that in mind when comparing summaries from different
                    sources is important in data analysis.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                <term>Sources:</term> TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                Nairobi city profile.
                Available at:
                <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
                Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA) (2013).
                <em>The Project on Integrated Urban Development Master Plan 
  for the City of Nairobi in the Republic of Kenya: Household Travel 
  Survey 2013.</em>
                Tokyo: JICA.
                Salon, D.
                and Gulyani, S.
                (2019).
                <q>Commuting in Urban Kenya: Unpacking Travel Demand in Large and 
  Small Kenyan Cities.</q>
                <em>Sustainability</em>, 11(14), 3823.
                <url href="https://doi.org/10.3390/su11143823"/>.
                CC BY 3.0.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-ch9-stack">
        <title>Variability STACK Question</title>

        <p>
            TODO: Insert STACK question on variability here.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-measuring-variability-r">
        <title>Measuring Variability in R</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                The previous section introduced the idea of variability and why
                it matters.
                This section works through how to measure it, using example.
                We will start with the data itself, then build up through
                several measures of variability, each one revealing something
                slightly different about the spread of the data.
            </p>

            <p>
                We use the <c>gapminder</c> package, which is already installed
                from <xref ref="sec-gapminder"/>.
                We filter to African countries only:
            </p>

            <program language="r">
                <code>
        library(tidyverse)
        library(gapminder)

        africa &lt;- gapminder %&gt;% filter(continent == "Africa")

        head(africa)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 6
        #&gt;   country continent  year lifeExp      pop gdpPercap
        #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;     &lt;int&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Algeria Africa     1952    43.1  9279525     2449.
        #&gt; 2 Algeria Africa     1957    45.7 10270856     3014.
        #&gt; 3 Algeria Africa     1962    48.3 11000948     2551.
        #&gt; 4 Algeria Africa     1967    51.4 12760499     3247.
        #&gt; 5 Algeria Africa     1972    54.5 14760787     4183.
        #&gt; 6 Algeria Africa     1977    58.0 17152804     4910.
                </code>
            </program>

            <p>
                Each row is one country in one year.
                The variable we will focus on is the life expectancy at birth,
                in years ( <c>lifeExp</c> ).
                A quick summary shows some statistics on the variability:
            </p>

            <program language="r">
                <code>
        summary(africa$lifeExp)
        #&gt;    Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max.
        #&gt;   23.60   42.37   47.79   48.87   54.41   76.44
                </code>
            </program>

            <p>
                The minimum life expectancy across all African countries and
                years is 23.6 years; the maximum is 76.4.
                The mean is 48.9.
                But those three numbers alone do not tell you much about the
                shape of the variation.
                That is what the rest of this section is for.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="ss-variance-sd">
            <title>Variance and Standard Deviation</title>

            <p>
                The most common way to measure variability is the
                <term>standard deviation</term>.
                This single number that summarises how spread out the values are
                around the mean.
                Before we get to standard deviation, it helps to understand
                <term>variance</term>, which is what standard deviation is built
                from.
            </p>

            <p>
                Both variance and standard deviation give an average of how far
                each data point sits from the mean.
                Variance computes this by taking the average of the squared
                distances from the mean.
                Standard deviation then takes the square root of the variance,
                bringing the answer back into the same units as the original
                data, making it much easier to interpret.
            </p>

            <p>
                We can visualise what variance actually means using the African
                life expectancy data.
                The same variance visualisation for 1952 and 2007 are shown side
                by side in <xref ref="fig-ch9-variance-squares"/>.
                Here, each country is shown as a point, with their respective
                life expectancy for that year on the y axis.
                The horizontal line in each panel sits at the mean life
                expectancy for that year, and each square's area represents the
                squared distance of one country from that mean.
                Four countries are highlighted in orange to illustrate different
                cases.
            </p>

            <p>
                Each square's side length equals the distance between that
                country's life expectancy and the mean, so its area equals the
                squared distance.
                The variance Life Expectancy in 2007 is simply the average area
                of all squares (grey and orange combined) in the right hand
                panel.
                Countries far from the mean contribute large squares; countries
                close to the mean contribute small ones.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-variance-squares">
                <caption>
                    Variance visualised for African life expectancy in 1952
                    (left) and 2007 (right).
                    Each square's area equals the squared distance from that
                    country's life expectancy to the continental mean
                    (horizontal line).
                    Variance is the average area of all squares.
                    Four countries are highlighted in orange: Botswana, Kenya,
                    Libya, and Swaziland.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_variance_squares.png" width="100%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            Two side-by-side scatter plots of African countries
                            ordered by life expectancy on the x-axis, with life
                            expectancy in years on the y-axis.
                            Grey and orange squares connect each country's point
                            to a horizontal mean line.
                            In the left panel (1952), the mean sits at around 39
                            years and the squares are relatively small and
                            clustered together, reflecting low variance.
                            Botswana appears above the mean with a notable
                            orange square; Kenya, Libya, and Swaziland are all
                            close to the mean with small squares.
                            In the right panel (2007), the mean has risen to
                            around 55 years and the squares are dramatically
                            larger overall.
                            Botswana now sits close to or below the mean.
                            Kenya remains close to the mean just below it.
                            Libya sits well above the mean with a large orange
                            square.
                            Swaziland sits far below the mean at around 39 years
                            with by far the largest orange square in the panel,
                            illustrating its enormous contribution to the
                            overall variance.
                            Several unlabelled grey squares from countries above
                            the mean are also large, reflecting the countries
                            that improved dramatically between 1952 and 2007.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Several things are immediately visible.
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The squares are dramatically larger in 2007 than in
                            1952.
                            This shows that the variance has increased
                            substantially, without any calculation needed.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The mean line has shifted upward, from around 39
                            years in 1952 to around 55 years in 2007.
                            Countries improved overall, but they spread apart as
                            they did so.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The four highlighted countries tell very different
                            stories.
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Life Expectancy in Botswana was far
                                        above the mean in 1952.
                                        Life Expectancy increased in Botswana
                                        from 1952 to 2007, but, at a slower rate
                                        than the continental average increase.
                                        Hence, in 2007, the Life Expectancy in
                                        Botswana was below the continental
                                        average.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Kenya sits close to the mean in both
                                        years, just slightly below it in 2007 —
                                        its square is moderate.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Life Expectancy in Libya was comparable
                                        to Kenya in 1952, but in 2007 sits well
                                        above the mean with a large orange
                                        square.
                                        It improved substantially while many
                                        other countries did not, pulling it far
                                        from the average.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Swaziland sits far below the mean in
                                        2007, with by far the largest orange
                                        square in the right panel.
                                        Its contribution to the overall variance
                                        is enormous — a single country whose
                                        square dwarfs those of most others
                                        combined.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
                We can now put numbers on what the image shows.
                The table below gives the standard deviation and variance of
                African life expectancy for each year from 1952 to 2007:
            </p>

            <program language="r">
                <code>
    africa %&gt;%
      group_by(year) %&gt;%
      summarise(SD       = round(sd(lifeExp), 2),
                variance = round(var(lifeExp), 2))
    #&gt; # A tibble: 12 x 3
    #&gt;     year    SD variance
    #&gt;    &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;
    #&gt;  1  1952  5.15     26.5
    #&gt;  2  1957  5.62     31.6
    #&gt;  3  1962  5.88     34.5
    #&gt;  4  1967  6.08     37.0
    #&gt;  5  1972  6.42     41.2
    #&gt;  6  1977  6.81     46.4
    #&gt;  7  1982  7.38     54.4
    #&gt;  8  1987  7.86     61.8
    #&gt;  9  1992  9.46     89.5
    #&gt; 10  1997  9.10     82.9
    #&gt; 11  2002  9.59     91.9
    #&gt; 12  2007  9.63     92.8
                </code>
            </program>

            <p>
                The standard deviation starts at 5.15 years in 1952 and nearly
                doubles to 9.63 years by 2007.
                The numbers confirm what the image showed: the squares got
                bigger.
                But the table also reveals something the two-panel image does
                not: the spike around 1992.
                The standard deviation jumps from 7.86 in 1987 to 9.46 in 1992 —
                its largest single-year increase in the entire dataset.
                The figure below shows why.
            </p>

            <p>
                The three panels show our 1952 and 2007 plot, now with 1992 in
                it (centre).
                All three have Rwanda highlighted.
                In the centre panel, the picture changes completely.
                Rwanda's orange square dominates the panel.
                This is a single country whose life expectancy had collapsed to
                23.6 years in the period surrounding the genocide, producing a
                squared distance from the mean so large that it pushed the
                variance up more than the cumulative increase of the previous
                decade.
                The standard deviation then dips slightly to 9.10 in 1997 as
                Rwanda begins to recover, before rising again as Swaziland and
                other southern African countries are pulled downward by HIV/AIDS
                (right panel).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-rwanda-variance">
                <caption>
                    Variance visualised for African life expectancy in 1952
                    (left), 1992 (centre), and 2007 (right).
                    Rwanda's enormous orange square in the centre panel
                    illustrates how a single outlier can dominate the variance.
                    By 2007, Rwanda has recovered toward the mean, but other
                    countries, particularly in southern Africa, have been pulled
                    downward by HIV/AIDS.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_rwanda_variance.png" width="100%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            Three side-by-side scatter plots of African
                            countries ordered by life expectancy.
                            The left panel shows 1952, with the mean at around
                            52 years and grey squares of moderate size.
                            The centre panel shows 1992: a large orange square
                            extends from Rwanda's point at around 23.6 years
                            down to the bottom of the panel, dwarfing all other
                            squares and pulling the mean line down slightly.
                            The right panel shows 2007, where Rwanda's orange
                            point sits close to the mean and has a small square,
                            reflecting its recovery.
                            Several other countries now have large grey squares
                            above and below the mean, reflecting the growing
                            spread caused by the HIV/AIDS epidemic in southern
                            Africa.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Variance is sensitive to outliers precisely because squaring the
                distances gives disproportionate weight to points far from the
                mean.
                Rwanda's squared distance from the mean in 1992 is enormously
                larger than its simple distance: Squaring amplifies it.
                This means the variance captures the full impact of extreme
                values, but it also means that a single unusual country can
                dominate the summary statistic — as Rwanda did in 1992, and
                Eswatini did in 2007.
                We will return to this when we discuss the interquartile range,
                which is designed specifically to be robust to outliers like
                these.
            </p>

            <p>
                We can visualise the increase in standard deviation:
            </p>

            <program language="r">
                <code>
    africa %&gt;%
      group_by(year) %&gt;%
      summarise(SD = sd(lifeExp)) %&gt;%
      ggplot(aes(x = year, y = SD)) +
        geom_line(colour = "#44575E", linewidth = 1) +
        geom_point(colour = "#44575E", size = 3) +
        labs(
          title = "Standard deviation of life expectancy across African countries",
          subtitle = "By year, 1952 to 2007",
          x = "Year",
          y = "Standard deviation (years)"
        ) +
        theme_bw()
                </code>
            </program>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-sd-over-time">
                <caption>
                    Standard deviation of life expectancy across African
                    countries, 1952 to 2007.
                    The overall trend is upward, reflecting growing divergence
                    between countries.
                    The sharp spike in 1992 reflects Rwanda; the sustained high
                    values from 1992 onward reflect the HIV/AIDS epidemic in
                    southern Africa.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_sd_over_time.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A line chart with year on the x-axis and standard
                            deviation in years on the y-axis.
                            The line rises steadily from around 5.15 in 1952 to
                            around 7.86 in 1987, then spikes sharply to 9.46 in
                            1992 before dipping slightly to 9.10 in 1997 and
                            rising again to 9.63 in 2007.
                            The overall trend is clearly upward throughout the
                            period.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The line illustrates the standard deviation across all twelve
                years at once.
                There is a steadily widening spread, with some countries
                diverging as they improved life expectancy at a faster pace than
                others, and others not improving.
                This is punctuated by two sharp events: Rwanda in 1992, and the
                sustained impact of HIV/AIDS from the mid-1990s onward.
                Standard deviation captures all of this in a single number per
                year, but the number only makes sense if you know what is
                driving it.
                That is why the squares matter: they show you not just the size
                of the variance, but where it is coming from.
            </p>
        </subsection>
        
        <subsection xml:id="ss-cv">
            <title>Coefficient of Variation</title>

            <p>
                The standard deviation is useful for comparing variability
                within the same dataset.
                But what if you want to compare variability across datasets with
                very different means?
                A standard deviation of nine years means something very
                different if the average life expectancy is 50 years versus 80
                years.
            </p>

            <p>
                The <term>coefficient of variation</term> (CV) solves this by
                expressing the standard deviation as a percentage of the mean.
                A CV of 20% means that, on average, values deviate from the mean
                by about 20% of the mean's value.
                This makes it possible to compare the relative spread of very
                different datasets.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
                <code>
        africa %&gt;%
          group_by(year) %&gt;%
          summarise(CV = sd(lifeExp) / mean(lifeExp) * 100)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 12 x 2
        #&gt;     year    CV
        #&gt;    &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
        #&gt;  1  1952  13.2
        #&gt;  2  1957  13.6
        #&gt;  3  1962  13.6
        #&gt;  4  1967  13.4
        #&gt;  5  1972  13.5
        #&gt;  6  1977  13.7
        #&gt;  7  1982  14.3
        #&gt;  8  1987  14.7
        #&gt;  9  1992  17.6
        #&gt; 10  1997  17.0
        #&gt; 11  2002  18.0
        #&gt; 12  2007  17.6
                </code>
            </program>

            <p>
                We previously saw that the standard deviation roughly doubled
                from 1952 to 2007, but here the CV only increased from 13.2% to
                17.6%.
                This is because the mean life expectancy also increased
                substantially over the same period.
                Part of what looked like growing variability in the standard
                deviation was simply that the whole distribution shifted upward.
                The CV adjusts for this, showing that the relative spread grew
                more modestly.
            </p>

            <p>
                However, limitations to the CV are that it assumes the data is
                roughly symmetric around the mean, and it behaves poorly when
                the mean is close to zero.
                For life expectancy data, which is always positive and not too
                skewed, it works well.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="ss-boxplot-variability">
            <title>Visualising Variability: the Boxplot</title>

            <p>
                Variance, standard deviation, and CV are all
                <term>point statistics</term>: single numbers that summarise the
                spread of the data.
                While useful, they do not show the shape of the distribution:
                whether values cluster in the middle or spread evenly, whether
                the data is skewed, or whether there are unusual outliers.
            </p>

            <p>
                A <term>boxplot</term> shows all of this at once.
                It displays the five-number summary with the minimum, first
                quartile (Q1), median, third quartile (Q3), and maximum as a box
                with whiskers.
                It additionally marks any outliers as individual points.
                Here is the boxplot for life expectancy across African countries
                in 2007:
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-africa-boxplot-2007">
                <caption>
                    Life expectancy across African countries in 2007, shown as a
                    boxplot with the five-number summary labelled.
                    The dot below the lower whisker is Swaziland (now Eswatini),
                    whose life expectancy was severely reduced by the HIV/AIDS
                    epidemic.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_africa_boxplot_2007.png" width="70%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A vertical boxplot showing life expectancy in years
                            on the y-axis.
                            The box spans from Q1 (57.2 years) to Q3 (76.4
                            years), with a median line at 71.9 years.
                            Labelled arrows point to each of the five summary
                            statistics.
                            A single outlier point appears below the lower
                            whisker, labelled as the minimum value (39.6 years).
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Reading this boxplot: the median life expectancy across African
                countries in 2007 was around 71.9 years.
                In 2007, the life expectancy in African countries varied
                considerably: half of all countries fell between roughly 57.2
                and 76.4 years (the box).
                The whiskers extend to the most extreme non-outlier values.
                The single dot below the lower whisker is Eswatini (now
                Eswatini) at 39.6 years, driven almost entirely by the HIV/AIDS
                epidemic at its peak.
                This is a real event, visible as an outlier because its cause
                was so severe and so specific to that country.
            </p>

            <p>
                We can verify which country is at the bottom:
            </p>

            <program language="r">
                <code>
        africa_2007 &lt;- gapminder %&gt;% filter(continent == "Africa", 
                                             year == 2007)

        africa_2007 %&gt;%
          arrange(lifeExp) %&gt;%
          select(country, lifeExp) %&gt;%
          head(5)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 5 x 2
        #&gt;   country                  lifeExp
        #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;                      &lt;dbl&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Swaziland                   39.6
        #&gt; 2 Mozambique                  42.1
        #&gt; 3 Zambia                      42.4
        #&gt; 4 Sierra Leone                42.6
        #&gt; 5 Lesotho                     42.6
                </code>
            </program>

            <insight xml:id="insight-outliers">
                <title>What to do with outliers?</title>

                <p>
                    As always, it depends on the context and the cause - like
                    with missing data.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If an outlier is a genuine data error, such as having an
                    adult with a height of 162m, then it is good practice to
                    verify what the correct value should be and fix it.
                    If it cannot be verified, it is reasonable to remove it from
                    the dataset.
                    But, removing an outlier simply because it is inconvenient
                    or makes the data look messy is not good practice.
                </p>

                <p>
                    In October 1987, a weather forecaster on the BBC dismissed a
                    concerns about an approaching storm, saying there was
                    nothing to worry about.
                    Hours later, the most destructive storm to hit southern
                    England in three centuries caused billions of pounds of
                    damage and killed eighteen people.
                    The forecasting models had flagged an unusual pressure
                    reading, but this was treated as noise rather than as real.
                    The outlier was the story.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Eswatini's life expectancy in 2007 is an outlier in the same
                    sense: not a data error, but a real event with a real cause.
                    Removing it would make the distribution look tidier and the
                    standard deviation smaller.
                    But, it would erase the HIV/AIDS epidemic in Eswatini from
                    the data.
                    The right response to an outlier is always to ask why it is
                    there.
                    It can be an error, but it can be one of the most
                    interesting parts of the dataset.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                We can look at how variability changed across years, displaying
                the same boxplot repeated for each year, with Kenya highlighted:
            </p>

            <program language="r">
                <code>
kenya &lt;- africa %&gt;%
  filter(country == "Kenya")

ggplot(africa, aes(y = lifeExp, x = factor(year))) +
  geom_boxplot(width = 0.4, fill = "#F37021", colour = "#44575E",
               outlier.colour = "#F37021", outlier.size = 2) +
  # Kenya points
  geom_point(data = kenya,
             aes(y = lifeExp, x = factor(year)),
             colour = "#44575E", size = 3, shape = 18) +
  # Kenya line connecting the points
  geom_line(data = kenya,
            aes(y = lifeExp, x = as.numeric(factor(year))),
            colour = "#44575E", linewidth = 0.8) +
  # Label on the last point only
  geom_label(data = kenya %&gt;% filter(year == max(year)),
             aes(y = lifeExp, x = factor(year), label = "Kenya"),
             hjust = -0.1, size = 3.2, fill = "white",
             colour = "#44575E", label.size = 0.3) +
  labs(
    title    = "Life expectancy across African countries",
    subtitle = "Each point represents one country. Kenya highlighted in dark teal.",
    y        = "Life expectancy (years)",
    x        = "Year"
  ) +
  theme_bw() +
  theme(
    panel.grid.major.x = element_blank(),
    panel.grid.minor.x = element_blank()
  )
                </code>
            </program>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-africa-boxplot-years">
                <caption>
                    Life expectancy across African countries from 1952 to 2007,
                    with Kenya shown as a connected dark teal line.
                    The widening of the boxes over time reflects growing
                    divergence between countries.
                    Kenya's dip in the 1990s reflects the HIV/AIDS epidemic.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_africa_boxplot_years.png" width="100%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A series of boxplots, one every five years from 1952
                            to 2007, showing life expectancy across African
                            countries.
                            The boxes gradually widen over time, consistent with
                            the increasing standard deviation shown in the table
                            above.
                            A dark teal connected line and diamond points show
                            Kenya's trajectory, rising steadily until the 1990s,
                            then dipping before recovering in 2002 and 2007.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The widening boxes over time tell the same story as the
                increasing standard deviation, but now you can visualise it.
                The median life expectancy across African countries rises
                steadily from 1952 through to the late 1980s/90s, then stalls
                and dips.
                In the late 1980s, the boxes start to plateau, and in the late
                1990s, the whiskers begin to stretch further downward,
                potentially from the impact of the HIV/AIDS epidemic.
                In 1992, a single orange point appears far below the rest.
                This is Rwanda, whose life expectancy had collapsed to around 23
                years, the lowest of any country in the dataset.
                This was the period surrounding the Rwandan genocide of 1994,
                and the violence and displacement of the years immediately
                before it.
                It registers in the data as a stark outlier, a single point
                sitting far beneath every other country on the continent.
                We saw this same point when we animated the Gapminder data in
                <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> and confirmed
                this variability is not a data error.
            </p>

            <p>
                Kenya's trajectory, shown by the dark teal line, shows
                concretely how an individual country's experience can reflect
                broader continental trends.
                Life expectancy rises consistently from 1952 to 1987, tracking
                close to the continental median.
                Then it falls, steeply, through the 1990s before recovering in
                the 2000s.
                By 2007, Kenya is back above the continental median, but the dip
                is visible in the data as clearly as it was in lived experience.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="ss-range">
            <title>The Range</title>

            <p>
                The simplest measure of variability is the <term>range</term>:
                the difference between the largest and smallest values in the
                dataset.
                In R:
            </p>

            <program language="r">
                <code>
      africa %&gt;%
        group_by(year) %&gt;%
        summarise(range     = max(lifeExp) - min(lifeExp),
                  max_lifeExp = max(lifeExp),
                  min_lifeExp = min(lifeExp))
      #&gt; # A tibble: 12 x 4
      #&gt;     year range max_lifeExp min_lifeExp
      #&gt;    &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;
      #&gt;  1  1952  21.4        52.7        31.3
      #&gt;  2  1957  22.1        58.1        36.0
      #&gt;  3  1962  26.9        60.2        33.3
      #&gt;  4  1967  24.2        64.4        40.2
      #&gt;  5  1972  26.1        65.0        38.8
      #&gt;  6  1977  28.9        67.1        38.2
      #&gt;  7  1982  33.4        70.0        36.7
      #&gt;  8  1987  36.5        71.9        35.4
      #&gt;  9  1992  52.5        73.6        23.6
      #&gt; 10  1997  40.9        74.8        36.1
      #&gt; 11  2002  43.1        75.7        39.2
      #&gt; 12  2007  40.0        76.4        39.6
                </code>
            </program>

            <p>
                The range is easy to understand and easy to compute.
                In 1952, the gap between the country with the highest life
                expectancy and the lowest was 21.4 years.
                By 2007 that had grown to 40 years.
                In other words, in 2007 one country within Africa had a life
                expectancy more than 40 years longer than another.
            </p>

            <p>
                The increasing range can be visualised in
                <xref ref="fig-ch9-africa-boxplot-years"/> too, where the gap
                between the top and bottom of each boxplot widens noticeably
                from left to right
            </p>

            <p>
                Look at 1992: the range jumps to 52.5 years.
                That is almost entirely Rwanda, a single country whose life
                expectancy had collapsed to 23.6 years, far below every other
                country on the continent, pulling the minimum down and the range
                up dramatically.
                By 1997, once the immediate crisis had passed, the range fell
                back to 40.9.
            </p>

            <p>
                This illustrates the main weakness of the range: it depends
                entirely on the two most extreme values.
                One unusual country (such as Rwanda in 1992, or Eswatini at the
                height of the HIV/AIDS epidemic) can dramatically change the
                range, even if every other country in the dataset is clustered
                tightly together.
                The range tells you about the extremes, not about the typical
                spread.
                This is why the interquartile range was developed.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="ss-iqr">
            <title>The Interquartile Range</title>

            <p>
                The box in a boxplot has a specific width: it spans from Q1 to
                Q3.
                That width is called the <term>interquartile range</term> (IQR).
                It measures the spread of the middle 50% of the data, which is
                the range within which the central half of all values fall.
            </p>

            <p>
                The IQR is more <term>robust</term> than the standard deviation,
                meaning it is less affected by outliers.
                Eswatini's extreme value pulls the standard deviation up, but it
                has no effect on the IQR because the IQR only cares about the
                middle half of the data.
                This makes the IQR a better measure of variability when you have
                skewed data or known outliers that you do not want to dominate
                the summary.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
                <code>
        africa %&gt;%
          group_by(year) %&gt;%
          summarise(IQR = IQR(lifeExp),
                    SD  = sd(lifeExp))
        #&gt; # A tibble: 12 x 3
        #&gt;     year   IQR    SD
        #&gt;    &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
        #&gt;  1  1952  7.44  5.15
        #&gt;  2  1957  7.33  5.62
        #&gt;  3  1962  8.08  5.88
        #&gt;  4  1967  8.25  6.08
        #&gt;  5  1972  8.96  6.42
        #&gt;  6  1977  9.89  6.81
        #&gt;  7  1982 11.8   7.38
        #&gt;  8  1987 12.3   7.86
        #&gt;  9  1992 13.9   9.46
        #&gt; 10  1997 13.4   9.10
        #&gt; 11  2002 13.5   9.59
        #&gt; 12  2007 14.5   9.63
                </code>
            </program>

            <p>
                Both the IQR and SD tend to increase over time, consistent with
                growing divergence between African countries.
                But notice that the IQR is generally larger than the SD in the
                early years, and smaller in the later years.
                This reflects the growing influence of the outliers in the 1990s
                and 2000s which pull the SD up more than the IQR, because the SD
                is sensitive to their extreme values while the IQR is not.
            </p>

            <p>
                We can visualise this increase in the IQR by looking at
                <xref ref="fig-ch9-africa-boxplot-years"/>.
                The boxes get taller over time because Q1 and Q3 move further
                apart.
                The IQR in 1952 was around 7 years; by 2007 it had almost
                doubled to 14.5.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="ss-least-squares">
            <title>Least Squares: Measuring Variability Around a Line</title>

            <p>
                So far, we have measured variability around a single value.
                But sometimes the question is not "how spread out are the
                values?" but "how well does a line describe the relationship
                between two variables?" The answer involves measuring
                variability around a line rather than around a point.
            </p>

            <p>
                Consider Kenya's population over time.
                There is a clear upward trend, but the data does not fall
                perfectly on a straight line.
                Each observed value sits some distance above or below the line.
                Those distances are called <term>residuals</term>.
                The plot below shows them as orange arrows:
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-kenya-residuals">
                <caption>
                    Population in Kenya, 1952 to 2007, with a line of best fit.
                    Orange arrows show the residual for each year (the distance
                    between the observed value and the line).
                    Years where the actual population was below the line have
                    arrows pointing upward; years above the line have arrows
                    pointing downward.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_kenya_residuals.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A scatterplot of Kenya's population by year, with a
                            dark teal line of best fit and orange arrows
                            connecting each observed data point to the fitted
                            line.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The goal of <term>least squares</term> is to find the line that
                makes those arrows (residuals) as short as possible, so that the
                line is as close to the data as possible.
                Specifically, it minimises the sum of the <em>squared</em>
                residuals.
                Why squared?
                First, squaring makes all residuals positive (so positive and
                negative errors do not cancel each other out).
                Second, squaring gives more weight to large residuals: a point
                that is far from the line contributes much more to the total
                than a point that is close.
                This means the line is pulled toward fitting the most extreme
                deviations, rather than ignoring them.
            </p>

            <p>
                The squared residuals can be visualised literally as squares.
                The side length of each square equals the residual for that
                point; the area equals the squared residual.
                The line of best fit is the one that minimises the total area of
                all the squares combined:
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-least-squares-comparison">
                <caption>
                    Left: a flat horizontal line through the mean, representing
                    a poor fit, producing large squares.
                    Right: the line of best fit, which minimises the total area
                    of the squares.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_least_squares_comparison.png" width="100%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            Two side-by-side plots of Kenya's population by
                            year.
                            In the left panel, a horizontal line sits at the
                            mean population, and large orange squares connect
                            each observed point to that line.
                            This is particularly large for the early and late
                            years.
                            In the right panel, the line of best fit slopes
                            upward, and the orange squares are substantially
                            smaller.
                            The total area of the squares in the right panel is
                            much less than in the left.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The animation below shows this process in action: the line
                rotating from horizontal to the line of best fit, with the
                squares shrinking and the total squared error falling as it
                moves:
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-least-squares-animation">
                <caption>
                    Animation showing the line rotating from a flat horizontal
                    line (maximum squared error) to the line of best fit
                    (minimum squared error).
                    The total squared error is shown in the subtitle and
                    decreases as the line improves.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_least_squares.gif" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            An animated GIF showing Kenya's population data
                            points with a line rotating from horizontal to the
                            line of best fit.
                            Orange squares shrink as the line rotates.
                            The subtitle updates to show the decreasing total
                            squared error.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="ss-variability-summary">
            <title>Choosing a Measure of Variability</title>

            <p>
                We have now seen several measures of variability.
                They are not interchangeable — each captures something slightly
                different, and each has situations where it is the right choice
                and situations where it is not.
            </p>

            <tabular halign="left">
                <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
                <col halign="left" width="25%"/>
                <col halign="left" width="25%"/>
                <col halign="left" width="30%"/>
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Measure</cell>
                    <cell>What it captures</cell>
                    <cell>Sensitive to outliers?</cell>
                    <cell>When to use</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell><term>Variance</term></cell>
                    <cell>Average squared distance from the mean</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>
                        When you need a mathematically convenient form; rarely
                        reported directly
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell><term>Standard deviation</term></cell>
                    <cell>
                        Average distance from the mean, in original units
                    </cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Symmetric data without extreme outliers; most common
                        default
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell><term>Coefficient of variation</term></cell>
                    <cell>SD as a percentage of the mean</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Comparing variability across datasets with different
                        means or units
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell><term>IQR</term></cell>
                    <cell>Spread of the middle 50% of the data</cell>
                    <cell>No</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Skewed data or data with meaningful outliers you do not
                        want to dominate
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell><term>Least squares</term></cell>
                    <cell>Variability around a line</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>
                        When measuring how well a trend or relationship fits the
                        data
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>

            <p>
                In practice, you will often report more than one.
                A boxplot gives the IQR visually; the standard deviation gives a
                single number for the spread; the CV lets you compare across
                groups.
                None of them is complete on its own, and the right choice
                depends on what you are trying to communicate and what your data
                looks like.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-ch9">
        <title>Exercises</title>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-variability-measures">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Using the <c>gapminder</c> package in R, filter the data to
                    a year of your choice and compute the following for life
                    expectancy across all countries:
                </p>

                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The range
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The standard deviation
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The interquartile range
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The coefficient of variation
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>

                <p>
                    Now repeat the exercise for each continent separately using
                    <c>group_by(continent)</c>.
                    Which continent has the highest standard deviation?
                    Which has the highest IQR?
                    Are they the same continent?
                    Why might they differ?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-variability-outlier">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In the Africa life expectancy data, Rwanda in 1992 appears
                    as an extreme outlier with a life expectancy of 23.6 years.
                </p>

                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Compute the standard deviation and IQR of African
                            life expectancy in 1992, with and without Rwanda
                            included.
                            How much does each measure change?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Which measure is more affected by removing Rwanda:
                            the standard deviation or the IQR?
                            Does this match what you would expect given the
                            properties of each measure?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Should Rwanda be removed from the dataset for
                            analysis purposes?
                            Justify your answer.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-variability-boxplot">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Produce a boxplot of life expectancy for all African
                    countries in two different years of your choice.
                </p>

                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Describe what changed between these two years in
                            terms of the median, the IQR, and the presence of
                            outliers.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The standard deviation in one year is higher than in
                            the other year.
                            Does the boxplot suggest this is because values are
                            more spread out overall, or because of specific
                            outliers?
                            How can you tell?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Add Kenya's data point to both boxplots using
                            <c>geom_point()</c>.
                            In which year is Kenya further from the continental
                            median, and in which direction?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</chapter>